To align the caption of a table to the left without using the `caption` package in LaTeX, you can use the basic commands provided by LaTeX itself, specifically the `\captionsetup` macro provided by the `caption` package. However, since you prefer not to use the `caption` package, you'll need a different approach.

You can instead use the `\\captionsetup{justification=raggedright,singlelinecheck=false}` directive from within the `table` environment to achieve left alignment of the caption text. However, note that this still technically uses the `caption` package but it adheres to your requirement if specifically loading the `caption` package is the concern.

Here’s a basic example demonstrating how to achieve this:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{caption}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[ht]
  \captionsetup{justification=raggedright,singlelinecheck=false}
  \caption{This is a sample caption for the table aligned to the left.}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
    \hline
    Column 1 & Column 2 \\
    \hline
    Data 1 & Data 2 \\
    Data 3 & Data 4 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

This document sets up a table with the caption aligned to the left side by setting `justification=raggedright` to align the caption text to the left, and `singlelinecheck=false` to make sure that the alignment setting applies even if the caption is a single line.

If you are looking for solutions strictly without using any external packages (including avoiding `\usepackage{caption}`), you could manipulate usual LaTeX commands for a somewhat manual adjustment. For instance, use the `tabular` environment within the `table` and explicitly align your caption using normal text alignment controls:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[ht]
  \begin{flushleft}
    \textbf{Table 1:} \emph{This is a sample caption for the table aligned to the left.}
  \end{flushleft}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
    \hline
    Column 1 & Column 2 \\
    \hline
    Data 1 & Data 2 \\
    Data 3 & Data 4 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

In this example, `\begin{flushleft}... \end{flushleft}` is used to align the caption text to the left manually. Note that this method will not automatically number your tables, and it somewhat strays from conventional LaTeX best practices for handling captions. However, it does the job without the need for additional packages. 

Either choice (minimal or no use of the `caption` package) depends on your specific limitations and requirements in your LaTeX document.

